            <ArticleTitle>Chronic whiplash pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>359-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmr.2015.01.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1047-9651(15)00004-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although most patients recover from acute whiplash injuries, those with chronic whiplash syndrome develop signs of central nervous system (CNS) amplification of pain and have a poor prognosis. In this context, specific pain generators from acute whiplash have been identified through clinical, biomechanical, and animal studies. This article gives a clinical perspective on current understanding of these pain generators, including the phenomenon of CNS sensitization.</AbstractText>
